    {
      "section": "article.story-header",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "logo": {
          "assetRole": "customer.logo",
          "alt": "Halcyon",
          "ref": "/assets/marquee/logo-6.svg"
        },
        "title": "Halcyon gives every trading desk self-serve data on a governed Lattice platform",
        "stats": [
          {
            "value": "4-6 billion",
            "label": "market events processed per trading day"
          },
          {
            "value": "70",
            "label": "analysts and engineers shipping reviewed models each week"
          }
        ],
        "quote": "Before Lattice, a question from a desk head could take a week to answer because three teams had to agree on where the data came from. Now there is one tested definition, one place to look it up, and most desks answer their own questions before lunch.",
        "name": "Grace Okafor",
        "role": "Chief Data Officer"
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "stroke.draw-on",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "render-final-state"
      },
      "style": {
        "strokeShape": "stroke.s5",
        "strokeGradient": "gradient.01"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "article.story-body",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "meta": {
          "logo": {
            "assetRole": "customer.logo",
            "alt": "Halcyon",
            "ref": "/assets/marquee/logo-6.svg"
          },
          "facts": [
            {
              "label": "Industry",
              "value": "Financial markets"
            },
            {
              "label": "Headquarters",
              "value": "Rotterdam, Netherlands"
            },
            {
              "label": "Founded",
              "value": "1998"
            }
          ]
        },
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "A market operator whose reporting had outgrown its spreadsheets and nightly scripts"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "[Halcyon](/case-studies) runs trading venues and clearing services for equities and commodities across nine European markets. Every trading day its systems record several billion market events, and every one of them can end up in a regulatory filing, a client report or an internal risk dashboard."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "For years that reporting ran on a patchwork of scheduled scripts, spreadsheets and a legacy warehouse that only two engineers fully understood. It worked, mostly. But each new regulatory requirement meant another script, and every desk had quietly built its own version of the same core figures, which made reconciling them a recurring and expensive chore."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "When the company set out to modernise its data stack, the goal was simple to state and hard to achieve: one trusted version of every number."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "The starting point: knowledge that lived in a handful of heads"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Opportunity 1: let desks help themselves"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The first opportunity was speed. Desk heads and product managers asked questions faster than the central team could answer them. A typical request involved finding the right tables, checking with the team that owned them, writing a one-off query and then explaining the caveats. By the time an answer arrived, the decision it was meant to inform had often been made already."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Grace Okafor, Halcyon’s Chief Data Officer, wanted the people closest to each market to be able to answer routine questions on their own."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Opportunity 2: prove where every regulated figure comes from"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The second opportunity was trust. Regulators increasingly asked Halcyon to show how reported figures were derived. With logic spread across scripts and spreadsheets, producing that evidence took weeks of manual work for every audit."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Okafor’s team needed lineage they could show to an auditor: a clear, versioned record of how raw events became a published figure."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Both goals pointed at the same foundation."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "One governed project instead of many scripts"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Choosing a platform that analysts could actually use"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Halcyon evaluated several approaches, from building in-house tooling to adopting a full data platform."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The team chose Lattice because analysts could write models in plain SQL while engineers kept the review, testing and deployment controls they needed."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Migration started with the most-used figures: daily traded volumes, settlement exposure and fee revenue. Each was rebuilt as a set of staged and modelled tables, tested, documented and reviewed by the desk that relied on it most."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Tests that encode the rules of the market"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Alongside standard checks on keys and nulls, the team wrote tests for the rules every trader knows by heart: trades never settle before they execute, fees never exceed agreed caps and every instrument maps to exactly one venue."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Those tests now run on every change and every nightly build. When a source system delivers something unexpected, the build stops and the owner is alerted before any report goes out."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Reviews as standard"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Every change to a production model goes through a pull request with at least one approval from someone outside the author’s team."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Reviewers see which downstream reports are affected and a sample of changed rows before they approve, which has turned reviews from a formality into a genuine safety net."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Lineage for auditors"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Because every model, test and change lives in version control, lineage is no longer reconstructed by hand. It is generated from the project itself."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "For the last two audits, Halcyon answered lineage requests in hours rather than weeks, with no findings on change control."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "From a legacy warehouse to a modern stack, without stopping the business"
          },
          {
            "kind": "img",
            "image": {
              "assetRole": "figure.article-image",
              "alt": "Diagram: sources flowing through staging and mart models to regulatory, assistant and board consumers",
              "ref": "/assets/pages/story/halcyon-flow.svg"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Running old and new side by side for a quarter"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Halcyon could not afford a big-bang migration. For three months the legacy scripts and the new Lattice models ran in parallel, and a reconciliation model compared their outputs every night."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Differences were triaged daily. Most turned out to be long-standing quirks in the old scripts, such as time-zone handling around market close, which the team documented and fixed rather than reproducing."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "When a figure matched for thirty consecutive trading days, its old script was switched off and the new model became the source of truth."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "By the end of the quarter, more than four hundred scripts had been retired."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Making lineage, documentation and ownership part of daily work"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Every model now has a named owner and a one-line description, and both are checked automatically during review."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Analysts browse the catalog to find certified tables before writing anything new, which has sharply reduced duplicate work across desks."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The documentation site is now the first place new joiners go. Onboarding that used to take a quarter now takes a few weeks."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "“The catalog changed how people ask questions,” says Okafor. “They start by looking for an answer that already exists, and most of the time it does.”"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Desk-level dashboards were rebuilt on top of [certified metrics](/product/semantic-layer), so every desk sees the same definition of volume, exposure and revenue."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Self-serve answers for every desk and product team"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Metrics people can find"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Halcyon’s semantic layer now holds around two hundred certified metrics, each with an owner, a description and tests."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Desk analysts query those metrics directly from their spreadsheet and reporting tools, without writing SQL or waiting for the central team."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Fewer tickets, faster answers for the people who make decisions"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Requests to the central data team fell by more than half within six months."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "These days most of the team’s hours go into fresh data products, such as intraday risk views, rather than repeating the same extracts every week."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "An assistant that reads the same definitions as the people using it"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Halcyon has started piloting an internal assistant connected to its Lattice project through the context service."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Because the assistant reads the same certified metrics and descriptions as analysts do, its answers match the numbers on the dashboards."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Every question the assistant answers is logged with the models and metrics it used, which the governance team reviews weekly."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Early users report that the assistant is most helpful for finding the right table and explaining unfamiliar columns."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The pilot will expand to all desks next year, with the same review and permission rules as the rest of the platform."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "What comes next for Halcyon’s data platform"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "With the core migration complete, Halcyon is turning to the next set of problems: bringing intraday data into the same governed project, and using state-aware builds to keep compute costs flat as volumes grow."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Intraday data in the same project"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The team is extending the project to cover intraday risk, using event-based triggers so models rebuild as soon as new data lands."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The same tests and reviews will apply, so intraday figures carry the same guarantees as end-of-day ones."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "First intraday views are already live for two desks."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h4",
            "text": "Keeping costs flat while data grows"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "State-aware builds already skip most unchanged models on a typical night. As volumes grow, the team expects that to keep compute spend roughly flat."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Cost per model is now reviewed monthly alongside quality metrics, with owners accountable for both."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "“We used to argue about where numbers came from,” says Okafor. “Now we argue about what to build next. That is a much better argument to have.”"
          }
        ]
      },
